#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:5
#: 3faea32eb74b45578d284db0ccf724c4
msgid "Starting up and calibrating Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:7
#: fc1a2884ada84be69e97dd7591631430
msgid "This article describes the basic setup and calibration that should be performed before launching Plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:11
#: 1f00e2c982a34554bbcadf5aa9d19be2
msgid "Ground calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:13
#: 574eaf51a2d9452894b601e7193151f7
msgid "Set your transmitter mode switch to :ref:`Manual <manual-mode>`. This is a safe mode in which to start up the system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:16
#: fb3dd3e31ffd41ee97b3d2266d654acf
msgid "When you power on your board at the field, you should leave the plane motionless on the ground until the :ref:`LEDs <common-leds-pixhawk>` stop flashing blue and red (about 30 seconds). This means that the gyros have been calibrated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:21
#: e957d42abc7644d29ab44b009dd8284c
msgid "Disengage the safety switch (if present)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:23
#: 6a57f02ea5ea49e19b3054f090dd1716
msgid "If the autopilot has a :ref:`safety switch <common-safety-switch-pixhawk>` connected, it must be disengaged before the vehicle can be :ref:`armed <arming-your-plane>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:25
#: 2a5d9bf1427d4efe88554d4c7059e24c
msgid "**Safety Switch LED Indications:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:27
#: f4e4ac18fcde453d83c655f678ead438
msgid "Fast Blinking indicates: Error Condition, Safety cannot be disengaged. Possibly not calibrated or sensor error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:29
#: 72608a9ccdba40f9b1e5efdc02c60787
msgid "Slow Blinking indicates: Safe condition. Safety can be disengaged by depressing Safety Button for 5 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:31
#: 106d93598f274f71bce58b73c08afd5c
msgid "LED Continuously on indicates: Safety has been disengaged. The autopilot may be armed with Throttle down and to the right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:34
#: 3ea9b6fe89c441d3ad5075cf95b0173b
msgid "When the LED is continuously on indicating Safety Disengaged it may be toggled back to a Safety engaged condition by depressing the Safety button for 5 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:40
#: 7461a08c58474acebe5beb7fc1ef8221
msgid "Both the Safety engaged and Safety disengaged conditions require the button to be held down for 5 seconds to toggle them. This is a safety mechanism to prevent accidental disarming during flight and accidental arming during transportation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:46
#: 3836b840090d47afa01810f1ca162379
msgid "Calibrate and check the Airspeed sensor (if present)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:48
#: ca62b2e7591a41e19e3f8c1deff0a7be
msgid "If the vehicle has an :ref:`airspeed sensor <airspeed>` then the :ref:`pre-flight checks described here <calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor>` should be performed before each flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:50
#: e55cf5b58ac8466fa33bb72115e8b769
msgid "Be sure that the pitot ports are lightly covered during boot and/or forced pre-flight calibrations to prevent wind from causing a mis-calibration. And be sure to remove that cover before flight!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:57
#: 0b132c1cf15646d48e5333432c7f0300
msgid "Wait for GPS lock"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:59
#: aadd5655d90b4a63827ced150c7759eb
msgid "You should wait for GPS lock before flying which is normally indicated by the autopilot's :ref:`LEDs <common-leds-pixhawk>` becoming green. If you do not wait for GPS lock the barometric altimeter calibration will be incorrect and :ref:`RTL <rtl-mode>`, :ref:`Loiter <loiter-mode>`, :ref:`Auto <auto-mode>` and any other GPS dependent modes will not work properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:62
#: 3f846f99ab1941059810ce70221b755b
msgid "For Plane the home position is initially established at the time the plane acquires its GPS lock. It is then continuously updated as long as the autopilot is disarmed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:66
#: 38327bf2638541ec9e6a3c18477b1286
msgid "This means that if you execute an RTL, your plane will return to the location at which it was armed. If the place you arm is not a good return point then please setup a :ref:`rally point <common-rally-points>` instead. A rally point will be used in preference to the home location for RTL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:72
#: b4fe2222ea044f01a34e96cb61b1ef46
msgid "Check servo movements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:74
#: 82ea2ba2860c4cf0bf006b6fcb7cea2c
msgid "**BEFORE EVERY FLIGHT**: before take off, hold the aircraft in your hands and switch to :ref:`FBWA <fbwa-mode>` mode, then pitch and tilt the plane to confirm that the control surfaces move the correct way to return it to level flight. (The ailerons and elevators will move; the rudder only coordinates turns with the ailerons in flight, so it won't move much on the ground). This will ensure that you haven't accidentally reversed a channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:82
#: ed645e4ffff24ccfafe06cbbb5542c4e
msgid "You should do this before every flight, just as you move your control surfaces with your RC transmitter to ensure that nothing's reversed. \\ **Failing to do this is the #1 cause of crashes.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:88
#: 0851cfa46a7f4b4da694b42e136b329b
msgid "As a safety measure, your throttle will only arm on the ground in Manual mode, Stabilize or for an autotakeoff in Auto mode. It will not come on in any other Auto mode until you are in motion in the air."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:97
#: c7a766adf4554b6ca2152f671d884564
msgid "First flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:99
#: 29634a16f99040a5a333ef8f17bee52a
msgid "It is highly recommended that you switch into either Stabilize or Fly By Wire mode and observe the behavior of the control surfaces. They should move to return the plane to level when you pitch or roll it. If it isn't rock solid, you can tune the gains by following the instructions \\ :ref:`here <common-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:105
#: df126092ad54447dba3c9e7fd054f44e
msgid "If you have not tuned your PID gains then you may like to consider doing the first takeoff in :ref:`AUTOTUNE <autotune-mode>` mode. That will start the tuning process as soon as you takeoff."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:110
#: 9f47b1e763e442f4a05e743f62347dfc
msgid "Second flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:112
#: dc108cd88e254233af01269f127fa32a
msgid "For your second flight, change the third mode (position 3 of your RC mode switch) to RTL in the Mission Planner's \\ :ref:`mode setup page <common-rc-transmitter-flight-mode-configuration>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:115
#: e0a4fdf7e4e9458abeb358b0ece53620
msgid "Once in the air switch the vehicle into RTL.  This will test navigation. The aircraft should return to the location at which it armed (or the nearest Rally point) and orbit at a fixed altitude (which can be set with the \\ :ref:`Mission Planner <planner:install-mission-planner>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:118
#: 8f87db4eb8ff41089374a38d10bb1fe4
msgid "If it does not return crisply and circle overhead in a near-perfect circle, you need to tune the autopilot a bit for your particular airframe. This can usually be done by adjusting the Roll parameters, as described :ref:`here <roll-pitch-controller-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:123
#: 2d66a1b84b7d4e618040cce8cd615077
msgid "Once all this has checked out, you can program waypoint missions and test them in Auto mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:127
#: 736441c7306d48639eb36fe95fcb957e
msgid "Level Adjustment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:129
#: 7103ea33037346be9c7bdabe17dab621
msgid "You may find after flying your plane in FBWA at the desired cruise throttle setting, that it does not hold level attitude with the transmitter sticks centered. If this happens, perform the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:131
#: 851fd8be07244e75ae6ce087d5707050
msgid "1) With your autopilot powered on the ground and connected to your mission planner, select FBWA on your transmitter, select the FLIGHT DATA tuning window and plot the nav_roll and nav_pitch data. With your transmitter sticks centered, these should both be zero as shown in this screenshot. If they are not, you need to repeat your RC calibration or adjust your transmitter trims and repeat the FBWA flight test"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:141
#: 4488da19399844c889e07d10681fa17d
msgid "If they are zero, then you need to adjust the level trim for the difference in angle between the autopilot board and your planes attitude when flying straight and level, as explained below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:144
#: 74d3e899b806454280291454c4d7ca0f
msgid "For roll errors (ie heading keeps changing with sticks neutral), use the \"LEVEL\" accelerometer calibrate button in Mission Planner with the wings level using a spirit level and the nose to tail fuselage line of the aircraft also level. Unless the plane is warped this will assure that the wings are level in flight. If the aircraft still rotates in heading with sticks neutral, then you will need to trim the rudder, if equipped, also in flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/starting-up-and-calibrating-arduplane.rst:146
#: 05866df34ea94aaba0c99540c5fc3c51
msgid "If the plane is not generally holding altitude in FBWA at the desired cruise throttle position (ie speed), then you can adjust the cruising pitch attitude using the :ref:`PTCH_TRIM_DEG<PTCH_TRIM_DEG>` parameter. Most planes required this to be a few degrees positive for best cruise trim in FBWA. This parameter is in centi-degrees, so 3 degs is 300 for its value."
msgstr ""
